@@s001 Covariance and correlation
@@s002 dato:one person,due:the means,ora:deviations from the mean,ok:positive product,no:negative product
@@s003 The data of the exercise (week 9): height and weight of 10 people. By eye: whoever is taller weighs more. Covariance turns this into a number.
@@s004 The means: <b>x̄ = 172.5</b> cm and <b>ȳ = 66</b> kg. The two lines divide the plane into 4 quadrants.
@@s005 For each person one looks at the <b>deviations from the mean</b>. For (195, 80): 195 − 172.5 = 22.5 and 80 − 66 = 14. Their product is <b>+315</b>.
@@s006 In the top right and bottom left quadrants the two deviations have the same sign: product <b>positive</b>. In the other two, <b>negative</b>. Here all 10 points are in the positive quadrants.
@@s007 Sum of the 10 products = 1440; divided by n − 1 = 9: <b>Cov = 160</b>. Divided by the standard deviations (15.14 and 10.63): <b>Corr = 0.995</b>, almost a line. Numbers equal to those of the notebook.
@@s008 ora:variances (diagonal),dato:covariances,ok:correlations,spento:to look at
@@s009 dato:y = x²,no:the line that correlation "sees"
@@s010 Covariance and correlation
@@s011 Lecture 8 and the exercises of week 9 (<i>Covariance and correlation</i>, Grasple on covariance and correlation vs causality). How to measure whether two variables change together, and why the same matrix returns in PCA. Exam question <b>Q7 (a)</b>. DW chapter 18.
@@s012 The idea, on a drawing
@@s013 Height and weight of 10 people
@@s014 The two formulas
@@s015 Mean of the products of the deviations. Positive: they rise together. Negative: when one rises the other falls. Close to 0: no <b>linear</b> relation.
@@s016 The covariance divided by the two standard deviations: a number without units of measure. ±1 = points exactly on a line.
@@s017 A variable with itself: that is why on the diagonal of the covariance matrix there are the variances and on that of the correlation all 1s.
@@s018 The covariance matrix
@@s019 With d variables all the pairs are computed: a <b>d × d</b> matrix, symmetric (Cov(X, Y) = Cov(Y, X)). With the data X (n × d) centred (the mean of each column removed): <span class="m">C = Xcᵀ Xc / (n − 1)</span> (the <a href="nozioni-dimensioni.html">dimensions</a>: (d × n)(n × d) → d × d).
@@s020 Covariance
