@@s038 Calculated by hand and compared with <code>np.cov</code> / <code>np.corrcoef</code> (equal) and with the output of the notebook. In covariance the height has the largest numbers only because it varies more (in cm); the correlation puts all the pairs on the same scale.
@@s039 Task 3 without ready-made functions (verified)
@@s040 The data are centred: from each column its mean is removed (<a href="nozioni-numpy.html">axis=0</a>, broadcasting).
@@s041 A single product of matrices computes all the sums Σ(xᵢ − x̄)(yᵢ − ȳ): the cell (j, k) is the inner product between column j and column k.
@@s042 The standard deviations are the roots of the diagonal. <code>np.outer(sd, sd)</code> is the matrix of all the products σⱼσₖ: one divides cell by cell.
@@s043 n − 1 or n?
@@s044 The exercise and <code>np.cov</code> divide by <b>n − 1</b> (the "unbiased" estimate); the DW notes divide by <b>n</b>. With n = 10 the height-weight covariance is 160 or 144. To get the DW version: <code>np.cov(X, rowvar=False, bias=True)</code>. With many data the difference disappears. And remember <code>rowvar=False</code> when the data are by rows (<a href="nozioni-numpy.html">NumPy</a>). The correlation does not change: the factor simplifies.
@@s045 Variances on the diagonal, then correlations
@@s046 Three traps
@@s047 What correlation does NOT say
@@s048 Covariance depends on the <b>units of measure</b>; correlation does not. To compare different pairs one uses correlation.
@@s049 y = x² with x symmetric about 0: Corr = <mark class="no">0.0</mark>
@@s050 y depends <b>completely</b> on x, but not linearly: the positive and negative deviations cancel out. Correlation 0 ≠ independence.
@@s051 Shoe and age are correlated (0.568) in these data, but the foot does not make one age. Often there is a third variable that moves both (a confounding factor), or it is chance of the small sample.
@@s052 Why it matters for machine learning
@@s053 Very correlated features carry almost the same information: a design matrix with almost dependent columns is unstable (<a href="w06-basi.html">bases</a>). The covariance describes the <b>shape</b> of the cloud of data: the variance in the direction of a unit vector v is <span class="m">vᵀCv</span>, and PCA looks for the directions where it is maximum (week 12). From the covariance comes also the Mahalanobis distance for <a href="w09-dati-mancanti-outlier.html">outliers</a>.
@@s054 Q7 (a)
@@s055 "Describe correlation and covariance, explain how they are calculated, and discuss how they are useful for understanding data and for descriptive analysis." To bring: the formula with the deviations (the drawing of the quadrants), the matrix and its diagonal, n vs n − 1, units of measure, the three traps, the link with PCA.
@@s056 Units, y = x², causality
